# Linux Estate — Vulnerability Assessment
### Executive Summary & Remediation Plan

**Scope:** Linux systems only, extracted from the Qualys vulnerability report. Windows, browser (Edge/Firefox), .NET, and Active Directory findings are out of scope for this analysis.
**Status:** Initial analysis. Host counts and several environmental details are *inferred from scan data* and require confirmation before remediation is scheduled — see **Unknowns**.

---

## 1. Executive Summary

The report surfaces roughly **2,700 Linux detections**, of which about **450 are rated Critical**. Those headline numbers overstate the actual body of work. Vulnerability scanners count every CVE on every host as a separate line-item, so a single missing kernel update generates dozens of entries, and one host that is a patch cycle behind can trip several hundred. Consolidated by root cause, the Linux estate resolves to a **small number of distinct problems replicated across an estimated 25–27 hosts.**

The estate divides into two populations that need to be handled differently:

- **Patchable now** — the great majority. AlmaLinux, Oracle Linux, and Ubuntu hosts are behind on standard OS patching. The overwhelming bulk of all detections, criticals included, is cleared by a routine `update + reboot` on each host. This is maintenance-window work, not project work.
- **Requires lifecycle / migration** — a smaller but higher-consequence set. The MySQL and Oracle Java estates are years behind, and MySQL in particular is running **end-of-life software that no longer receives security patches at all.** These cannot be "patched current"; they require version migration and belong in planning, not a patch window.

The single most important message for stakeholders: **the volume is not the risk. A disciplined patch sweep collapses the number dramatically, which then isolates a short list of genuinely serious items — led by the end-of-life MySQL estate — that warrant dedicated attention.**

### Estate at a glance

| Cohort | Est. hosts | Patch posture | Primary action |
|---|---|---|---|
| AlmaLinux | ~17 | 1–2 cycles behind | OS update + reboot |
| Oracle Linux (OEL) | ~8 | ~12 months behind (kernel & base) | OS update + reboot; confirm kernel stream |
| MySQL | ~8 (roles on above hosts) | **~4 years behind + End-of-Life** | **Migrate off 8.0 → 8.4 LTS** |
| MongoDB | ~3 (roles on above hosts) | ~1 version behind | Point-release upgrade |
| Oracle Java SE | ~1 (role on above host) | ~2 years behind | Inventory & update all JDKs; review licensing |
| Ubuntu | ~1–2 | Mixed | Kernel update + reboot |

*Note: the database and Java findings are **software roles running on the OS hosts above**, not additional machines. The true unique host count is driven by the OS cohorts (~17 Alma + ~8 OEL + ~1–2 Ubuntu).*

