## 2. The Worst Problems

Ranked by a combination of severity, exploitability score (QDS), remediation difficulty, and blast radius.

**1. End-of-Life MySQL 8.0 estate — highest priority.**
Flagged at maximum exploitability score (**QDS 100, Severity 5**) across ~8 hosts. MySQL 8.0 reached end of life in **April 2026**, so these databases now receive **no security updates whatsoever** — future vulnerabilities will never be fixed on this version. Compounding this, the same hosts carry open Critical Patch Updates going back to **2022**, meaning ~4 years of unapplied database patches. This is the estate's most serious exposure and the only item that cannot be resolved by patching. **Remediation is a migration to MySQL 8.4 LTS (or 9.x), scoped as a project.**

**2. Cross-fleet kernel vulnerability — "DirtyFrag" (CVE-2026-43284 / CVE-2026-43500).**
Appears in the outstanding kernel updates for **AlmaLinux, Oracle Linux, and Ubuntu** at **QDS 95**. Because it spans all three OS cohorts, it is the most widespread high-severity item in the report. It was the subject of a prior remediation effort, so the fix path is understood: the same kernel update that clears the rest of the base-OS backlog remediates it. Recommended as the anchor of the immediate patching push. *(Live exploitation status should be verified against CISA KEV — see Unknowns.)*

**3. Oracle Linux — ~12-month patch debt.**
The OEL cohort carries open errata dating to **2025**, including kernel updates, versus ~1–2 cycles for AlmaLinux. Open year-old kernel errata is the largest single block of patch debt in the estate. The remediation is routine (`update + reboot`), but the *degree* of lag suggests an underlying process gap (see Unknowns — patch source / subscription state), which matters more than the individual CVEs.

**4. Oracle Java SE — two QDS-95 criticals on ~1 host.**
Small in count but not in risk: Java SE is a historically heavily-targeted class of vulnerability, and this install is ~2 years behind. The host appears to carry **multiple JDK Critical Patch Updates simultaneously**, which usually indicates several Java installations layered on one machine (a system JDK plus JDKs bundled inside application directories). Remediation requires **inventorying every Java binary on the host**, not a single OS update, and should include a **licensing review** (Oracle JDK is subscription-licensed; a free OpenJDK/Temurin build may be the better fix).

**5. Samba (QID: QDS 95, AlmaLinux + Oracle Linux) — conditional.**
High exploitability score and remediated by the standard OS update. Priority escalates sharply **if any of these hosts serve files**; negligible if Samba is installed but unused. Confirming role is a five-minute check that changes the ranking.

*Also present and trivially fixed within the standard patch sweep, but worth naming to stakeholders because they get asked about: `sudo` (privilege-escalation, CVE-2026-35535) and `openssh` — both remote/privilege-relevant, both cleared by the OS update.*

