## 4. Unknowns — Questions to Resolve

This analysis is built entirely from a point-in-time scan. The following are **not yet known** and materially affect prioritisation and scheduling. Several should be answered before Phase 1.

**Host inventory and overlap.** Host counts throughout are *inferred* from detection-count patterns in the scan; the arithmetic is consistent but unconfirmed. A per-asset export is needed to establish the real inventory and to confirm whether the EOL-MySQL and patch-lagged-MySQL findings are the same ~8 hosts (assumed, not proven).

**Network exposure.** The scan does not indicate which hosts are internet-facing versus internal-only. A QDS-95 vulnerability on an exposed host is a different risk than the same finding on an isolated one. This is the single biggest driver of *real* priority and is not derivable from the data I have.

**Business criticality.** I don't yet know which hosts are production, which carry critical workloads, or which can tolerate a reboot/maintenance window and when. This determines the achievable pace of Phases 1–3.

**Patch source & subscription state (Oracle Linux especially).** How these hosts receive updates — subscription, public repositories, or automation-driven — is unknown, and is very likely *why* the OEL cohort drifted ~12 months. Some hosts may be unsubscribed or offline from patching entirely. Needs confirmation.

**Oracle Linux kernel stream (UEK vs RHCK).** OEL can boot either the Unbreakable Enterprise Kernel or the Red Hat-compatible kernel. Remediation must target the stream actually in use, or findings will not clear after reboot. A quick `uname -r` check across the cohort resolves this.

**Live exploitation status.** Several top items are 2026 CVEs. Their current real-world exploitation status and CISA KEV listing should be verified against live threat-intelligence sources to sharpen the "do first" ordering beyond the scanner's own score.

**Scan completeness.** Whether the scan was fully authenticated/credentialed is unconfirmed; an unauthenticated scan can under-report. Worth verifying so we know the ~2,700 figure is a floor, not a partial view.

**Backup / rollback posture.** Before any reboot or the MySQL migration, confirmed backups and a tested rollback path are prerequisites. Current state unknown.

